                         difficulty, answer_status, content_key, origin)
    VALUES (subj, tid, 'In a constant V/f induction motor drive, the slip at the maximum torque', 'In a constant V/f induction motor drive, the slip at the maximum torque
', '**Idea used:** slip at maximum torque $s_m = \dfrac{R_2}{X_2}$ (rotor resistance over rotor standstill reactance, stator impedance
neglected), and a reactance grows with frequency: $X_2 = 2\pi f L_2$.

1. Under V/f control the supply frequency $f$ is changed to change the speed. Synchronous speed $N_s = \dfrac{120f}{P}$, so $N_s \propto f$.
2. $R_2$ does not change with $f$, but $X_2 \propto f$.
3. So $s_m = \dfrac{R_2}{2\pi f L_2} \propto \dfrac{1}{f} \propto \dfrac{1}{N_s}$: an **inverse** relation.

**Good to know:** the maximum torque itself stays (nearly) constant under V/f control, and the *speed drop* at maximum torque,
$s_m N_s = \dfrac{R_2}{2\pi L_2} \times \dfrac{120}{P}$, is the same at every frequency. That is why the torque-speed curves just shift
sideways.

**Answer: (C).**
', 'READY', 'MCQ', NULL, NULL, 2, 'OFFICIAL_KEY', 'gate2016-s1-q16', 'PYQ') RETURNING id INTO qid;
